#quote-box[
#set text(fill: black)
Let $P(s)$ denote the plant transfer function. Draw the block diagram of a closed-loop system controlled with a PID controller.
The controller block should be detailed into its proportional, integral, and derivative actions in frequency domain form.
]
The block diagram of a closed-loop system controlled with a PID controller can be seen as :
#diagram(
  node-stroke: 0.8pt,
  edge-stroke: 0.8pt,
  node-corner-radius: 2pt,
  spacing: (14mm, 10mm),
  node(
    enclose: (<sum>, <pid>, <plant>, <tap>, <neg>, <fb1>, <fb2>),
    stroke: (paint: gray, dash: "dashed", thickness: 0.8pt),
    fill: none,
    corner-radius: 4pt,
    snap: false,
    label: align(bottom + right)[#text(rgb("#444444"))[$G_("ry")(s)$]],
    name: <box>
  ),
  node((0,0), $r(s)$, stroke: none, name: <r>),
  node((1,0), $+$, shape: circle, width: 7mm, name: <sum>),
  node((2.5,0),
    align(center)[
      $C(s)$ \
      #v(5pt)
      $= k_P + display(k_I/s) + display(k_D s)$
    ],
    width: 38mm, height: 20mm,
    name: <pid>
  ),
  node((4.5,0),
    $P(s)$,
    width: 18mm, height: 10mm,
    name: <plant>
  ),
  node((5.5, 0), stroke: none, name: <tap>),
  node((6.5, 0), $y(s)$, stroke: none, name: <y>),
  node((5.5, 1.1), stroke: none, name: <fb1>),
  node((1,   1.1), stroke: none, name: <fb2>),
  node((3.5, 1.1),
    $-1$,
    width: 12mm, height: 10mm,
    name: <neg>
  ),
  edge(<r>, <sum>, "-|>"),
  edge(<sum>, <pid>, "-|>", label: $e(s)$, label-pos: 0.3),
  edge(<pid>, <plant>, "-|>", label: $u(s)$, label-pos: 0.45),
  edge(<plant>, <tap>, "-"),
  edge(<tap>, <y>, "-|>"),
  edge(<tap>, <fb1>, "-"),
  edge(<fb1>, <neg>, "-|>"),
  edge(<neg>, <fb2>, "-"),
  edge(<fb2>, <sum>, "-|>"),
)

Where 

- $r(s)$: Reference (desired output).
- $e(s)$: Error signal, defined as $e(s) = r(s) - y(s)$, representing the difference between reference and actual output.
- $U(s)$: Input control signal generated by the controller to drive the system toward the reference.
- $P(s)$: Plant (system to be controlled), characterized by its transfer function $P(s) = y(s)/u(s)$.
- $C(s)$: Controller, with transfer function $C(s)$ that processes the error signal to produce $u(s)$.
- $y(s)$: System output, influenced by both the control input ,disturbances and noises.
- $G_(y r)(s)$: Closed-loop transfer function, relating the system output $y(s)$ to the reference input $r(s)$.
