] 


#theorem-box(title:
"From the block diagram described in Question 1, the signals are related as"
)[
$ cases(
  e(s)= r(s)-y(s),
  u(s)= e(s) C(s),
  y(s)= P(s) u(s)
) $ 
]
By substituing the definition to the plant output,
$
y(s)= P(s)e(s)C(s)=P(s)C(s)[r(s)-y(s)]
$
$
y(s)[1+P(s)C(s)]= P(s)C(s)r(s)
$
The closed loop transfer function is defined as 
$
G_(y r )= Y(s)/R(s)= (P(s)C(s))/(1+P(s)C(s))= frac(P(s)[k_P +k_d s + k_I s^(-1)],1+P(s)[k_P +k_d s + k_I s^(-1)])
$

= Question 3
/*
* 3 parameter well explained
* 1 parameters is not well explained or lacks of precision
* More thatn 1 parameter is not explained correctly or overall lack of details
*/
#quote-box[
#set text(fill: black)
Intuitively, what would be the effect of modifying each parameter ($k_P$, $k_I$, and $k_D$) independently?
]
=== Proportional Gain $k_P$ (Present)
Directly scales the error signal $e(s)$, providing immediate corrective action.
It counteracts the instantaneous value of the error.
=== Integral Gain $k_I$ (Past)
Accumulates past errors to eliminate steady-state (or static) error.
=== Derivative Gain $k_D$ (Future)
Anticipates future error trends by differentiating $e(s)$, damping oscillations.

= Question 4
/*
* - no good set was found + justification 
* - integral action small justification
*/
#quote-box[
#set text(fill: black)
In the following simulation, tune the proportional and integral gains $k_P$ and $k_I$.

a)  Give a set of values for which the actual angle converges towards the reference angle. In the case you can't find any, what's the apparent problem with the PI controller?
]
/*The PI controller cannot converge correctly, as $k_P$ and $k_I$ increases, the reaction time decrease  but create oscilations.It requires a parameter to anticipate how fast is the convergence torwards r and how fast the error decreases: a derivative gain $k_P$.
*/
The PI controller cannot achieve monotonic convergence. Increasing 
$k_P$  and $k_I$ speeds up the response, but the continuously‑integrated error keeps growing, producing persistent oscillations and overshoot that destabilise the system. In other words, without a derivative term to anticipate the error‑rate, the controller cannot settle on the reference angle.
The inclusion of a derivative term, 
 $k_D$, furnishes the necessary predictive action to attenuate the acceleration of the error. By opposing rapid variations of the error, the derivative contribution mitigates overshoot and curtails oscillatory behavior, thereby enabling the closed‑loop system to settle swiftly while maintaining proximity to the reference signal.

A refined set of tuning parameters that satisfies these requirements is approximately

$k_P≈34.5$ and
$k_I≈ 2 times 10^(-4)$
 (value selected to match the observed damping).
With this combination, the system exhibits rapid convergence and only bounded, well‑controlled oscillations around the desired angle, confirming that the addition of derivative feedback restores both stability and speed, whereas integral action alone degrades overall performance.

#figure(
  image("q4.svg",width:100%),
  caption:[refined solution with $k_P$=34.5361 and $k_I$=0.0002],
)
#quote-box[
#set text(fill: black)
 b) Does the integral action improve the behavior of the control system? Why?
 ]
The integral term can be useful because it eliminates any steady‑state offset that a pure proportional controller would leave behind. By continuously accumulating the tracking error, it drives the controller output toward a value that removes the residual error, thereby improving long‑term accuracy. However, in this cart‑pole system the accumulated error grows too aggressively, forcing the controller to over‑compensate and produce large control actions that push the pole past the upright position. Consequently, while the integral action can reduce steady‑state error, it also accelerates the response in a way that generates overshoot and ultimately destabilises the closed‑loop system. Hence, simply adding integral gain does not lead to a better overall performance unless it is carefully balanced with other controller components

