== Disturbance
#figure(
  image("q6_dist.svg",width:100%),
  caption:[ distortion enabled
  ],
)<disturb>
When a load disturbance acts on the input channel (@disturb), the proportional term ($k_D$) delivers a strong corrective effort that counteracts the added torque, limiting the amplitude of the resulting oscillations. The integral action ($k_I$) eliminates any steady‑state offset left by the disturbance, while the derivative action continues to add damping, ensuring that the system returns to the reference trajectory without excessive overshoot.

== Noise
#figure(
  image("q6_noise.svg",width:100%),
  caption:[ measurement noise enabled
  ])<noisy>
In the noise‑only (@noisy) scenario the controller first produces a modest oscillation because the proportional term ($k_P$) reacts to the low‑amplitude measurement perturbation with a limited corrective effort; the derivative action ($k_D$) initially suppresses the high‑frequency component, so the response remains small. As the noisy signal accumulates, the derivative term continues to generate a growing control effort that the integral action ($k_I$) integrates, gradually increasing the effective gain seen by the system. Once the integrated error reaches a threshold, the closed‑loop torque command amplifies the original disturbance, leading to the observed transition from a small‑amplitude oscillation to a large, sustained one. 

